--TEST--
`Core\Test::assertContains` holds exactly where `Core\Arr::contains` answers `true` — a haystack-by-needle grid counted rather than read off a row, membership by value and never by key, the numeric domain taken from `Core\Arr` too, and both sides named in the report — `rule:testing/assertions-are-typed`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `assertContains`'s subject is an `array<T>` and its needle that same `T`,
// which is `Core\Arr::contains`'s own signature -- `nvs_stdlib::test`'s doc
// comment owns why, and the consequence worth pinning is that the two members
// can never disagree about membership. So this counts agreement across a whole
// grid instead of asserting a verdict per row: a member that grew its own
// comparison still prints plausibly on any single line and fails here.

use Core\Test;

final class Ask {
    public static function contained(array<string> $subject, string $needle): string {
        try {
            Core\Test::assertContains($subject, $needle);
        } catch (Core\Test\Failure $fromContains) {
            return "failed";
        }
        return "held";
    }

    public static function reported(array<string> $subject, string $needle): string {
        try {
            Core\Test::assertContains($subject, $needle);
        } catch (Core\Test\Failure $fromReport) {
            return $fromReport->message;
        }
        return "held";
    }
}

// 1. The grid: five haystacks -- the empty one, a single entry, a list, a
// keyed map and a run of duplicates -- against five needles each. Both counts
// are asserted, so a member that held everywhere agrees with nothing.
array<array<string>> $haystacks = [
    [],
    ["a"],
    ["a", "b", "c"],
    ["x" => "a", "y" => "b"],
    ["a", "a", "a"],
];
array<string> $needles = ["a", "b", "c", "z", ""];

int $agreed = 0;
int $held = 0;
int $failed = 0;
foreach ($haystacks as array<string> $haystack) {
    foreach ($needles as string $needle) {
        string $verdict = Ask::contained($haystack, $needle);
        bool $memberSays = $verdict == "held";
        bool $arrSays = Core\Arr::contains($haystack, $needle);
        if ($memberSays) {
            $held = $held + 1;
        } else {
            $failed = $failed + 1;
        }
        if ($memberSays == $arrSays) {
            $agreed = $agreed + 1;
        }
    }
}

echo "agreed=", $agreed, "/25 held=", $held, " failed=", $failed, "\n";

// 2. Membership is over the *values*, so a key that looks like a needle is not
// one -- the half of the question a list-shaped haystack cannot ask.
array<string> $map = ["x" => "a", "y" => "b"];
echo Ask::contained($map, "a"), " ", Ask::contained($map, "x"), "\n";

// 3. The empty array holds nothing at all, including the empty string, and is
// a row rather than a refusal.
array<string> $empty = [];
echo Ask::contained($empty, ""), " ", Ask::contained($empty, "a"), "\n";

// 4. What "is the needle" means is `Core\Arr::contains`'s comparison and not a
// second one, so `rule:expressions/equality-semantics`'s numeric row reaches here: `int`, `uint`,
// `float` and `decimal` are one domain, and `[1.0]` therefore contains `1`.
array<float> $floats = [1.0, 2.5];
try {
    Test::assertContains($floats, 1);
    echo "held ";
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $numeric) {
    echo "failed ";
}
echo Core\Arr::contains($floats, 1) ? "true" : "false", "\n";

// 5. The report names both sides by name -- the reversed-argument insurance
// this whole class is written with -- and says how many entries were searched.
echo Ask::reported(["a", "b", "c"], "z"), "\n";
echo Ask::reported($empty, ""), "\n";

// 6. `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R2's trailing bag sits in front of that report as everywhere.
try {
    Test::assertContains($map, "seed", {message: "the fixture loaded its seed row"});
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $withMessage) {
    echo $withMessage->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
agreed=25/25 held=7 failed=18
held failed
failed failed
held true
Core\Test::assertContains failed: `$actual` holds 3 entries and none is `$expected`, which is "z"
Core\Test::assertContains failed: `$actual` holds 0 entries and none is `$expected`, which is ""
the fixture loaded its seed row: Core\Test::assertContains failed: `$actual` holds 2 entries and none is `$expected`, which is "seed"
